# The Tale of the First Echo

Long ago, when the world was newly formed, a young goddess named Lyra possessed the most beautiful voice in all creation. She would sing to the mountains, the rivers, and the sky, filling the world with melodies that made flowers bloom and stars shine brighter.

One day, Lyra fell deeply in love with a mortal musician named Cael. She would sing to him each evening, and he would play his lute in response. Their music together created harmonies so perfect that even the other gods wept with joy.

But mortals are fleeting, and when Cael's time came to pass into the afterlife, Lyra's grief was boundless. She climbed to the highest peak and sang a song of such profound sorrow that the mountains themselves cracked open. Her voice poured into every canyon, cave, and crevice, searching desperately for her beloved.

The gods took pity on Lyra and granted her a gift: whenever anyone calls out in loneliness or longing, the mountains return their voice, carrying it back to them. This is Lyra's song, forever searching for Cael, reminding us that love—even lost—never truly disappears.

And so, the echo was born from a goddess's eternal devotion.
